resurgam-worker (0.2.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * resurgam-lab-prober gains two new Level 2/3 check types, added live
    (2026-09-22) after a real, decisive finding on the first fully
    successful Windows Recovery Lab run: `icmp` failed outright (Windows
    Firewall's default profile blocks ICMP echo), but `ip neigh` showed
    the guest had answered ARP the entire time -- ARP resolution happens
    in the kernel below any userspace firewall, making it a more
    reliable "is this host actually up" signal than ICMP for exactly
    this common case.
    - `arp` (Level 2): triggers ARP resolution via a `ping` (its own
      ICMP result is ignored, only used as a trigger) then reads `ip
      neigh show`'s resulting state -- no new `arping` binary needed.
    - `nmap_scan` (Level 3): a bounded top-100-ports `nmap` scan (never
      an operator-suppliable range or nmap script, keeping with the "no
      arbitrary scripts" design), reporting real open-port evidence in
      the result detail. New dependency: `nmap`.

 -- RESURGAM <noreply@resurgam.invalid>  Tue, 22 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000

resurgam-worker (0.2.0-3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fixed a real bug found live (2026-09-22) in the same real Windows
    restore attempt that -2 targeted: `meta` (part of the original
    guest's real backed-up config, carrying `creation-qemu`/`ctime`
    housekeeping info) is not a valid `qm create` option at all --
    `qm help create` confirms it only exists as an unrelated sub-key of
    `--cicustom` -- so passing the original config through wholesale
    made `qm create` fail outright ("Unknown option: meta") before a VM
    was ever created. `lock`/`digest`/`parent` are the same class of
    informational/computed-state field and are now stripped
    defensively too, even though only `meta` has actually been observed
    live so far.

 -- RESURGAM <noreply@resurgam.invalid>  Tue, 22 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000

resurgam-worker (0.2.0-2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fixed a real, decisive bug found live (2026-09-22) during the first
    actual Windows Recovery Lab boot: `resurgam-lab-agent` built the
    member's VM config from scratch (memory/cores/net0/scsi0 only),
    hardcoding `scsi0` as the disk slot regardless of what the original
    guest actually used. It happened to boot a Linux guest that used
    virtio-scsi, but a real Windows guest (backed up on `sata0`) fell
    straight into Windows's repair/recovery mode -- confirmed via the
    real console. The synthetic config had also silently dropped
    `machine` type, NIC model, `ostype`, real socket/core/memory counts,
    and the `smbios1` UUID, any of which Windows can treat as a
    boot-critical hardware change.
    `resurgam-lab-agent` now fetches the *original* guest's real config
    from the backup itself (every PBS VM backup carries its own
    `qemu-server.conf.blob`, fetched via
    `proxmox-backup-client restore <snapshot> qemu-server.conf.blob -`)
    and reconstructs the lab VM from it wholesale -- only remapping the
    disk slot the mapped archive actually belongs to (derived from the
    archive's own filename, `drive-<slot>.img.fidx`) to the local COW
    overlay, and `net0`'s bridge to the run's isolated Lab VNet
    (preserving the original NIC model/MAC, dropping `firewall=1` since
    no rules exist for an ad-hoc VNet). Other disk/NIC slots from the
    original are dropped -- Phase A only ever maps one archive, and
    passing the rest through would reference production storage IDs the
    Lab cluster doesn't (or, worse, coincidentally does) have.

 -- RESURGAM <noreply@resurgam.invalid>  Tue, 22 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000

resurgam-worker (0.2.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Recovery Lab Phase B (3-tier probe hierarchy, ADR 0019): this source
    package is renamed from resurgam-lab-agent to resurgam-worker and
    now produces TWO binary packages from one tree -- resurgam-lab-agent
    (unchanged) and the new resurgam-lab-prober (the per-run probe
    appliance's own daemon + router/DHCP/firewall bootstrap).
  * resurgam-lab-agent gains a real bug fix found live (2026-09-21)
    while building this feature: `create_vm`'s `qm create` never passed
    `--agent 1`, so QEMU never exposed the guest-agent virtio-serial
    channel at all -- `POST .../agent/ping` always failed with "No QEMU
    guest agent configured" regardless of whether the guest OS actually
    had qemu-guest-agent installed. Confirmed against the first real
    Recovery Lab member VM this session booted (111), which had no
    `agent` key in its config at all.
  * resurgam-lab-agent also gains the write-side of a second
    responsibility: writing each Lab run's probe appliance cloud-init
    snippet to local disk (`pvesm path`), since PVE has no REST endpoint
    to upload a snippet file (confirmed live 2026-09-21 -- the upload
    API's `content` param rejects "snippets" outright, even with the
    storage's own content-type list including it).
  * resurgam-lab-prober is new -- build-verified (2026-09-22, same host
    as this session's earlier dh-virtualenv builds): both packages build
    cleanly and carry their expected content, confirmed via
    `dpkg-deb -c`. A real packaging bug found in the same pass:
    `dh_installsystemd --name=X` looks for `debian/<package>.X.service`,
    not `debian/X.service` -- the first attempt used the wrong filename
    for `resurgam-lab-router.service` (renamed to
    `debian/resurgam-lab-prober.resurgam-lab-router.service`) and,
    worse, passed a redundant `--name` for each package's own
    default-matching unit too, which silently installed *no* systemd
    units into *either* package. Fixed by dropping the redundant
    `--name` calls and using the documented filename convention for the
    one genuinely extra unit.
  * NOT yet live-verified against a real booted appliance -- in
    particular the router bootstrap script's interface-discovery-by-IP
    approach (`resurgam-lab-router-bootstrap`, finding the net0 guest
    interface by matching GATEWAY_IP rather than assuming a fixed name)
    and the dnsmasq/nftables config it generates need confirming against
    a real boot before trusting this package for an actual Lab run.

 -- RESURGAM <noreply@resurgam.invalid>  Tue, 22 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000

resurgam-lab-agent (0.1.0-4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fixed a second real bug found in the same live Recovery Lab run,
    immediately after the -3 fix let `map_snapshot` actually reach a
    real `proxmox-backup-client map` call: the "Image '...' mapped on
    /dev/loopN" confirmation line lands on the process's *stderr*, not
    stdout, on this PBS client build -- `map_snapshot` only ever
    searched `result.stdout` for the device path, so a genuinely
    successful map was always treated as a parse failure (leaving the
    device mapped and orphaned, since the code believed the map never
    happened). Now searches stdout first, falling back to stderr.

 -- RESURGAM <noreply@resurgam.invalid>  Mon, 21 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000

resurgam-lab-agent (0.1.0-3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fixed a real bug found during the first actual Recovery Lab run
    against a real PBS instance: `proxmox-backup-client` rejects
    `--repository` combined with a separate `--port` flag outright
    ("mutually exclusive"). The port now lives inside the repository
    string itself (`_pbs_repository`'s own PBS-native syntax,
    `[[user@]server[:port]:]datastore`) instead of being passed as
    `--port` in `pbs_client.map.map_snapshot`/`list_snapshot_archives`,
    which no longer accept a `port` parameter at all -- it was never
    usable together with `--repository` to begin with.

 -- RESURGAM <noreply@resurgam.invalid>  Mon, 21 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000

resurgam-lab-agent (0.1.0-2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Build-verified for real (Debian 13/trixie, amd64) -- two bugs the
    previous unverified packaging actually had:
    - debian/rules' dh_virtualenv override invokes the external
      `virtualenv` binary, not merely importable via Python -- missing
      from a bare debhelper/dh-virtualenv install (dh-virtualenv itself
      doesn't pull it in as a hard dependency on trixie). No fix needed
      in this repo -- document as a build-host prerequisite:
      `apt install virtualenv` alongside debhelper/dh-virtualenv.
    - debian/resurgam-lab-agent.install's second column is a destination
      *directory* in dh_install's syntax, not a destination path -- the
      original `... etc/default/resurgam-lab-agent` line installed the
      file nested one level too deep
      (etc/default/resurgam-lab-agent/resurgam-lab-agent.default) and
      broke dh_fixperms. Removed the file entirely: dh_installinit
      already auto-installs a file literally named
      debian/<package>.default to /etc/default/<package> by convention,
      making the explicit mapping both wrong and redundant.

 -- RESURGAM <noreply@resurgam.invalid>  Mon, 21 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000

